{"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"76070858","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.025996,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.482658+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.456662+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# Formula 1 Podium Prediction Companion\n","### Milestone 1\n","\n","**Team:** 124\n","\n","**Goal.** For every driver entering a Formula 1 race, predict whether they finish on the podium (`positionOrder ≤ 3`).\n","\n","**Prediction cutoff.** After qualifying, before the race starts. Anything that only becomes known once the race is running or finished (results, status, points, laps, pit stops, updated standings) cannot be used as a feature.\n","\n","**Unit of analysis.** One driver entering one race, so every row of the final modeling table is one `(raceId, driverId)` pair.\n","\n","**Dataset.** Formula 1 World Championship (1950–2024), Kaggle / Ergast, 14 linked CSV tables."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"9c4bd7aa","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.020018,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.663978+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.64396+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## Table of Contents\n","\n","1. **Data Cleaning & Auditing**\n","   - 1.0 Setup & Raw Data Load\n","   - Part A — Initial EDA (before cleaning)\n","   - Part B — Cleaning\n","   - Part C — EDA after cleaning\n","2. Data-Engineering Questions\n","3. Feature Selection & Engineering\n","4. Predictive Modeling\n","5. Experiments\n","6. Model Explainability (XAI)\n","7. Inference Function\n","8. Conclusions & Limitations"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7a4d9886","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.019779,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.70427+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.684491+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## The Problem and the Questions\n","\n","Before each race, once qualifying is over, we want to estimate for every driver how likely they are to finish in the top three. The information available at that moment is limited to what is known before the lights go out: the starting grid, qualifying, and how the driver and their team have performed in earlier races. Everything that happens during or after the race is the outcome, not an input.\n","\n","Two things make this harder than it looks. According to the F1 introduction, podiums are rare by design, about 3 of 20 drivers per race in the modern era and even fewer when fields were larger, so the positive class is small. And the dataset stores pre-race and post-race information side by side in the same tables, so the main risk is accidentally using the future to predict it.\n","\n","The data therefore needs to answer three kinds of questions:\n","\n","1) **What is actually available before the race?** Which tables and columns describe the pre-race situation, and how complete and reliable are they across 75 seasons?\n","2) **The three data-engineering questions from the brief:** front-row conversion by circuit before and after 2014, the home-race effect after controlling for grid, and mechanical retirement rates by constructor in 2014–2021 vs. 2022–2024.\n","3) **How well can pre-race information predict a podium, and what does the model rely on?** This is answered by the models, experiments and explanations in Sections 4–6."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"3acd5b8a","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.021071,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.7456+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.724529+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["---\n","# 1. Data Cleaning & Auditing\n","\n","## 1.0 Setup & Raw Data Load\n","\n","We load all 14 tables exactly as they are stored in the CSV files. By default pandas silently turns some strings (such as empty cells, \"NA\" or \"null\") into missing values while leaving others untouched, which would mix its decisions with ours before we have even looked at the data. Loading with `keep_default_na=False` keeps every value as written, so deciding what counts as \"missing\" becomes a step we take and document ourselves in Part B."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":1,"id":"5b536a17","metadata":{"_cell_guid":"b1076dfc-b9ad-4769-8c92-a6c4dae69d19","_uuid":"8f2839f25d086af736a60e9eeb907d3b93b6e0e5","execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.788243Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.787268Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.433216Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.432101Z"},"papermill":{"duration":9.670251,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.435767+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:06.765516+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["circuits                    77 rows    9 cols\n","constructors               212 rows    5 cols\n","constructor_results      12625 rows    5 cols\n","constructor_standings    13391 rows    7 cols\n","drivers                    861 rows    9 cols\n","driver_standings         34863 rows    7 cols\n","races                     1125 rows   18 cols\n","results                  26759 rows   18 cols\n","qualifying               10494 rows    9 cols\n","lap_times               589081 rows    6 cols\n","pit_stops                11371 rows    7 cols\n","sprint_results             360 rows   16 cols\n","seasons                     75 rows    2 cols\n","status                     139 rows    2 cols\n","\n","Seasons: 1950 - 2024\n"]}],"source":["import pandas as pd\n","import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n","\n","DATA_DIR = \"/kaggle/input/datasets/rohanrao/formula-1-world-championship-1950-2020/\"\n","\n","TABLES = [\"circuits\", \"constructors\", \"constructor_results\", \"constructor_standings\",\n","          \"drivers\", \"driver_standings\", \"races\", \"results\", \"qualifying\",\n","          \"lap_times\", \"pit_stops\", \"sprint_results\", \"seasons\", \"status\"]\n","\n","# raw tables, loaded exactly as stored. Never modified; cleaning works on copies.\n","raw = {name: pd.read_csv(DATA_DIR + name + \".csv\", keep_default_na=False) for name in TABLES}\n","\n","for name, df in raw.items():\n","    print(f\"{name:22} {df.shape[0]:>7} rows  {df.shape[1]:>3} cols\")\n","\n","print(\"\\nSeasons:\", raw[\"races\"][\"year\"].min(), \"-\", raw[\"races\"][\"year\"].max())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"c6d05552","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.020163,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.476389+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.456226+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["### Observations\n","\n","All 14 tables loaded, and the races run from 1950 to 2024. The `seasons` table has 75 rows, one for each year in that range.\n","\n","1) **results:** has 26,759 rows across 1,125 races, about 24 entries per race, assuming every race has results, which we still need to check. The F1 introduction describes a modern race as about 20 drivers and says fields in the 1950s–1980s were much larger, with non-qualifiers still getting a result row. Our hypothesis is that the extra rows come from those older, bigger fields. If that is true, the share of podium rows should be lower in those seasons, since every race still has only three podium places. We check this season by season in the EDA.\n","\n","2) **qualifying:** has only 10,494 rows, about 39% of the size of `results`. At roughly 20 drivers per race, that covers only around 500 races. This fits the brief's warning about \"historical qualifying-coverage drift\" and the introduction's statement that qualifying is only recorded in detail for recent decades. We need to find out exactly which seasons it covers before deciding how to use it.\n","\n","3) **sprint_results:** is small (360 rows), which fits sprints only existing from 2021, a handful per season. A sprint is a short race held on the same weekend but before the main race starts, so its result is already available at our prediction cutoff and, by the brief's definition of leakage, is not future information. Whether it is useful as a feature is a separate question: it only covers a few recent races, which we look at when building features.\n","\n","4) **lap_times** and **pit_stops:** describe what happened during the race, so by the brief's definition they cannot be features. `lap_times` is the largest table (589,081 rows), and we still need it in cleaning, because the brief asks us to convert lap-time strings into seconds.\n","\n","5) **driver_standings** and **constructor_standings:** are updated after each race, so the brief counts them as post-race information. According to the introduction, they only become usable if shifted to \"standings before this race\".\n","\n","Two things we want to check: whether every one of the 1,125 races actually has results, and which seasons qualifying covers. First, we open each table to see what its columns contain."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"55cafdae","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02036,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.517156+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.496796+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["---\n","## Part A — Initial EDA (before cleaning)\n","\n","## 1.1 Getting to Know the Tables\n","\n","Before measuring anything, we want to know what each table holds. We look at the first rows of each table, describe what every column means, and note anything that will need attention in cleaning and whether its information is available before the race starts, since that decides whether it can ever be used as a feature.\n","\n","We go through the tables in five groups, starting with the small tables that describe things and ending with the tables that refer to them, so that by the time we reach a table full of IDs we already know what each ID points to. How the tables actually link is verified in 1.6.\n","\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d2d4110c","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.021932,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.559386+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.537454+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":[" ## 1.1.1 **circuits**"]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":2,"id":"cc7c8eb5","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.602252Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.601629Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.656381Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.655446Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.078831,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.658604+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:16.579773+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["<class 'pandas.core.frame.DataFrame'>\n","RangeIndex: 77 entries, 0 to 76\n","Data columns (total 9 columns):\n"," #   Column      Non-Null Count  Dtype  \n","---  ------      --------------  -----  \n"," 0   circuitId   77 non-null     int64  \n"," 1   circuitRef  77 non-null     object \n"," 2   name        77 non-null     object \n"," 3   location    77 non-null     object \n"," 4   country     77 non-null     object \n"," 5   lat         77 non-null     float64\n"," 6   lng         77 non-null     float64\n"," 7   alt         77 non-null     int64  \n"," 8   url         77 non-null     object \n","dtypes: float64(2), int64(2), object(5)\n","memory usage: 5.5+ KB\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>circuitId</th>\n","      <th>circuitRef</th>\n","      <th>name</th>\n","      <th>location</th>\n","      <th>country</th>\n","      <th>lat</th>\n","      <th>lng</th>\n","      <th>alt</th>\n","      <th>url</th>\n","    </tr>