berg  1985-06-27   \n","3         4      alonso     14  ALO  Fernando      Alonso  1981-07-29   \n","4         5  kovalainen     \\N  KOV    Heikki  Kovalainen  1981-10-19   \n","\n","  nationality                                             url  \n","0     British     http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lewis_Hamilton  \n","1      German      http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nick_Heidfeld  \n","2      German       http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Nico_Rosberg  \n","3     Spanish    http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fernando_Alonso  \n","4     Finnish  http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Heikki_Kovalainen  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["df = raw[\"drivers\"]\n","df.info()\n","display(df.head())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"078a1458","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.022025,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.248767+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.226742+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["`drivers` has one row per driver, 861 in total:\n","\n","- **driverId:** the number that identifies each driver. Other tables use it to say which driver a row is about.\n","- **driverRef:** a short text name for the driver (e.g. `hamilton`), another identifier.\n","- **number:** the driver's permanent car number (e.g. 44 for Hamilton).\n","- **code:** a three-letter abbreviation of the driver's name (e.g. HAM).\n","- **forename / surname:** the driver's first and last name.\n","- **dob:** date of birth, written as text (e.g. 1985-01-07).\n","- **nationality:** the driver's nationality, written as an adjective (British, German, Spanish, Finnish).\n","- **url:** the driver's Wikipedia page.\n","\n","**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Types:** only `driverId` is a number. `number` is stored as text because it contains `\\N`: Heidfeld and Kovalainen have no permanent number, while Hamilton, Rosberg and Alonso do. Our guess is that permanent numbers only exist for more recent drivers, which the audit will measure. We do not need this column anyway.\n","\n","2) **dob** is a date stored as text, so it has to be converted to a real date in cleaning (semantic typing). It could be useful later: a driver's age on race day is known before the race starts.\n","\n","3) **nationality** confirms what the F1 introduction said: drivers' nationality is an adjective (\"British\"), while `circuits.country` uses country names (\"UK\"). For Question 2 we will need a mapping between the two, so we list all the nationality values below.\n","\n","4) **Available before the race?** Yes. A driver's identity, birth date and nationality are fixed facts known in advance. `forename` and `surname` are useful for readable output; `driverRef`, `code` and `url` are not needed for our questions, and we always identify drivers by `driverId`.\n","\n","**How are nationalities written?** To plan the mapping for Question 2, we list every distinct value."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":7,"id":"fa1574bc","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.302678Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.301722Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.311711Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.309654Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.044707,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.314493+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.269786+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["43 different nationality values\n","['American', 'American-Italian', 'Argentine', 'Argentine-Italian', 'Argentinian ', 'Australian', 'Austrian', 'Belgian', 'Brazilian', 'British', 'Canadian', 'Chilean', 'Chinese', 'Colombian', 'Czech', 'Danish', 'Dutch', 'East German', 'Finnish', 'French', 'German', 'Hungarian', 'Indian', 'Indonesian', 'Irish', 'Italian', 'Japanese', 'Liechtensteiner', 'Malaysian', 'Mexican', 'Monegasque', 'New Zealander', 'Polish', 'Portuguese', 'Rhodesian', 'Russian', 'South African', 'Spanish', 'Swedish', 'Swiss', 'Thai', 'Uruguayan', 'Venezuelan']\n"]}],"source":["print(df[\"nationality\"].nunique(), \"different nationality values\")\n","print(sorted(df[\"nationality\"].unique()))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"afd85a68","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.030442,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.376596+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.346154+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["There are 43 nationality values, and three things matter for Question 2:\n","\n","1) **The same nationality written two ways:** `Argentine` and `Argentinian ` (with a trailing space) describe the same nationality, so they would be counted as two. In cleaning we merge them into `Argentine`, the form also used in `Argentine-Italian`, and remove stray spaces from text values.\n","\n","2) **Dual nationalities:** `American-Italian` and `Argentine-Italian` combine two countries. When we answer Question 2 we have to decide which country counts as home for these drivers; the simplest rule is to count a race as a home race if it is held in either country. \n","\n","3) **Nationalities without a circuit:** some nationalities belong to countries that never hosted a race in our circuits list, or no longer exist (e.g. `Rhodesian`, `Liechtensteiner`). Drivers of these nationalities can never have a home race, which Question 2 has to take into account."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"352456b7","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.021506,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.425244+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:17.403738+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**How many East German drivers are there?** `East German` is a real historical nationality, not a spelling error, so we keep it in cleaning. Whether these drivers count as racing at home in Germany is a decision for Question 2, and its importance depends on how many drivers it concerns.\n","\n","Overall, drivers use 43 adjective forms while circuits use 35 country names (some as short forms like `UK`), so Question 2 needs a ma