 number  grid position  \\\n","0         1      18         1              1     22     1        1   \n","1         2      18         2              2      3     5        2   \n","2         3      18         3              3      7     7        3   \n","3         4      18         4              4      5    11        4   \n","4         5      18         5              1     23     3        5   \n","\n","  positionText  positionOrder  points  laps         time milliseconds  \\\n","0            1              1    10.0    58  1:34:50.616      5690616   \n","1            2              2     8.0    58       +5.478      5696094   \n","2            3              3     6.0    58       +8.163      5698779   \n","3            4              4     5.0    58      +17.181      5707797   \n","4            5              5     4.0    58      +18.014      5708630   \n","\n","  fastestLap rank fastestLapTime fastestLapSpeed  statusId  \n","0         39    2       1:27.452         218.300         1  \n","1         41    3       1:27.739         217.586         1  \n","2         41    5       1:28.090         216.719         1  \n","3         58    7       1:28.603         215.464         1  \n","4         43    1       1:27.418         218.385         1  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["df = raw[\"results\"]\n","df.info()\n","display(df.head())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"72192631","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.023217,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.857406+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.834189+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["`results` has 26,759 rows. From the first rows, each row looks like one driver's result in one race, which is our unit of analysis. The brief warns that this does not always hold: in early F1, drivers sometimes shared a car during a race, so the same driver can appear more than once in the same race. We investigate and fix this later.\n","\n","- **resultId:** a number identifying each row.\n","- **raceId / driverId / constructorId:** the race, the driver and the team the row belongs to.\n","- **number:** the car number.\n","- **grid:** the driver's starting position in the race.\n","- **position:** the official finishing position; it is empty if the driver was not classified.\n","- **positionText:** the finishing position written as text.\n","- **positionOrder:** a ranking of every driver in the race; our target is `positionOrder ≤ 3`.\n","- **points:** championship points scored in the race.\n","- **laps:** laps completed.\n","- **time / milliseconds:** the winner's total race time (`1:34:50.616`) and, for the others, the gap to the winner (`+5.478`); `milliseconds` holds each driver's total time as one number.\n","- **fastestLap / rank / fastestLapTime / fastestLapSpeed:** the lap on which the driver set their fastest lap, how that lap ranked in the race, its time and its average speed.\n","- **statusId:** why the driver's race ended (points to `status`).\n","\n","**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **The target and the grid are complete:** `positionOrder` and `grid` are whole numbers, so neither contains a placeholder. This supports the F1 introduction's statement that `positionOrder` is filled for every row, which is why our target uses it rather than `position`. `position` is stored as text even though the first rows show numbers, so some of its values are not numbers. The F1 introduction explains why: `position` is only filled for classified drivers. We look at the other rows below.\n","\n","2) **Starting position matters but does not decide everything:** in the first race shown, the top three started 1st, 5th and 7th, and the driver who finished 4th started 11th. So `grid` should be a strong feature, but not a perfect one: the F1 introduction lists car performance, driver skill, strategy, reliability and luck alongside starting position as what decides a race.\n","\n","3) **Almost everything here is post-race:** for the race a row describes, only a few columns are known before it starts.\n","\n","| Known before the race | Only known after the race (leakage) |\n","|---|---|\n","| `raceId`, `driverId`, `constructorId`, `number`, `grid` | `position`, `positionText`, `points`, `laps`, `time`, `milliseconds`, `fastestLap`, `rank`, `fastestLapTime`, `fastestLapSpeed`, `statusId` |\n","\n","`positionOrder` is also post-race, but it has a special role: it is where our target comes from (`positionOrder ≤ 3`), so it is never a feature.\n","\n","The right-hand columns can still be used for *earlier* races, for example to measure a driver's recent form, which is how we build leakage-safe features in Section 3.\n","\n","**What do `position`, `positionText` and `positionOrder` show when a driver is not classified?** We look at the rows where `position` is `\\N`."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":19,"id":"6441a623","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.909244Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.908638Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.931081Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.929811Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.050423,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.933278+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:18.882855+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Rows without an official position: 10953 of 26759\n","positionText\n","R     8897\n","F     1368\n","W      336\n","N      190\n","D      151\n","E        9\n","11       1\n","14       1\n","Name: count, dtype: int64\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","    