","114       sprint_results   fastestLapTime           9        0      0   \n","113       sprint_results       fastestLap           9        0      0   \n","84            qualifying               q1         156        0      0   \n","64               results           number           6        0      0   \n","\n","     pct_missing  \n","18          99.9  \n","59          98.7  \n","58          98.4  \n","55          95.3  \n","53          94.0  \n","51          94.0  \n","57          94.0  \n","54          93.6  \n","28          93.1  \n","50          92.0  \n","52          92.0  \n","56          92.0  \n","29          87.9  \n","72          71.3  \n","71          71.3  \n","76          69.2  \n","75          69.2  \n","73          69.2  \n","74          68.2  \n","86          65.4  \n","48          65.0  \n","85          44.3  \n","66          40.9  \n","112          5.6  \n","111          5.6  \n","106          4.2  \n","114          2.5  \n","113          2.5  \n","84           1.5  \n","64           0.0  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["def audit(tables):\n","    \"\"\"One row per column: how many values are \\\\N, empty, or already missing (NaN).\"\"\"\n","    rows = []\n","    for name, table in tables.items():\n","        for col in table.columns:\n","            n_sentinel = (table[col] == \"\\\\N\").sum()\n","            n_empty = (table[col] == \"\").sum()\n","            n_nan = table[col].isna().sum()\n","            n_missing = n_sentinel + n_empty + n_nan\n","            rows.append({\"table\": name, \"column\": col,\n","                         \"n_sentinel\": n_sentinel, \"n_empty\": n_empty, \"n_nan\": n_nan,\n","                         \"pct_missing\": round(100 * n_missing / len(table), 1)})\n","    return pd.DataFrame(rows)\n","\n","audit_before = audit(raw)\n","affected = audit_before[audit_before[\"n_sentinel\"] + audit_before[\"n_empty\"] + audit_before[\"n_nan\"] > 0]\n","affected = affected.sort_values(\"pct_missing\", ascending=False)\n","\n","print(\"Columns with missing values:\", len(affected), \"of\", len(audit_before))\n","display(affected)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d8710879","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.024077,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.114109+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.090032+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Where are the placeholders?** The plot shows, for every affected column, the share of rows that hold a placeholder."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":28,"id":"4ff1d616","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.169021Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.168527Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.537282Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.536266Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.398261,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.539719+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.141458+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 800x1000 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["labels = affected[\"table\"] + \".\" + affected[\"column\"]\n","\n","fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(8, 0.3 * len(affected) + 1))\n","ax.barh(labels, affected[\"pct_missing\"])\n","ax.invert_yaxis()\n","ax.set_xlabel(\"% of rows that are \\\\N or empty\")\n","ax.set_title(\"Placeholder values per column (raw data)\")\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()\n"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4175636d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.029969,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.598201+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.568232+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","The 30 affected columns fall into three groups, and only one of them matters for our features:\n","\n","1) **Columns we never use:** `constructor_results.status` is almost entirely empty (12,608 of 12,625 rows). The session schedule columns in `races` (`fp1_date` to `sprint_time`) are missing for 92–99% of races and `races.time` for 65%, and `drivers.number` and `drivers.code` are missing for most drivers (93% and 88%). None of these is needed for our questions or features, so their gaps cost us nothing.\n","2) **Post-race columns:** in `results`, `time` and `milliseconds` (71%), the fastest-lap columns (68–69%) and `position` (41%) are missing for many rows, and the same kind of columns in `sprint_results` for a few. They describe the outcome of the race, so they are never features. `position` being missing so often is why the target uses `positionOrder`, which does not appear in the plot because it is complete; neither does `grid`.\n","3) **Qualifying times:** `q1` is almost always filled (1.5% missing), while `q2` is missing for 44% of rows and `q3` for 65%. This is the knockout format from 1.1: a driver knocked out in Q1 or Q2 has no later time. These gaps are not errors; they tell us how far a driver got in qualifying, which we can use when building qualifying features in Section 3.\n","\n","**What the plot cannot show:** it measures gaps inside the rows that exist. Races with no qualifying rows at all do not appear here; we measure that coverage per season in 1.3."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"71680429","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.027642,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.658467+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.630825+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 1.3 Pre-Cleaning EDA: F1 Over Time\n","\n","Several questions from 1.1 depend on the season: whether older races really had larger fields, which seasons have qualifying data, what a grid position of 0 means, and how often the same driver appears twice in one race. We answer each with a per-season view. All columns used here (`year`, `grid`, `positionOrder`, `positionText`) are complete, so this can be done on the raw data.\n","\n","First, we attach the season to every result."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":29,"id":"94158d43","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.716545Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.716224Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.734931Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.733578Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.049624,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.736961+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.687337+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Rows before and after the join: 26759 26759\n","Races with results: 1125 of 1125\n"]}],"source":["# Attach the season to every result (results -> races through raceId)\n","res = raw[\"results\"].merge(raw[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n","print(\"Rows before and after the join:\", len(raw[\"results\"]), len(res))\n","print(\"Races with results:\", res[\"raceId\"].nunique(), \"of\", len(raw[\"races\"]))\n","\n","res[\"podium\"] = res[\"positionOrder\"] <= 3"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"8e5daebb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.025679,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.789084+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.763405+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Field size and podium share.** In 1.0 we found about 24 entries per race, more than a modern grid of 20, and expected older seasons to have larger fields. Since every race has only three podium places, the share of podium rows should then be lower in those seasons."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":30,"id":"d02fb397","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.8416Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.841271Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.229724Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.228662Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.417633,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.231747+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:21.814114+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1400x400 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["per_season = res.groupby(\"year\").agg(races=(\"raceId\", \"nunique\"),\n","                                     rows=(\"raceId\", \"size\"),\n","                                     podium_rows=(\"podium\", \"sum\"))\n","per_season[\"entrants_per_race\"] = per_season[\"rows\"] / per_season[\"races\"]\n","per_season[\"podium_share\"] = 100 * per_season[\"podium_rows\"] / per_season[\"rows\"]\n","\n","fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2, figsize=(14, 4))\n","per_season[\"entrants_per_race\"].plot(ax=axes[0])\n","axes[0].axhline(20, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\", label=\"modern grid (20)\")\n","axes[0].set_title(\"Average entrants per race\")\n","axes[0].set_ylabel(\"entrants\")\n","axes[0].legend()\n","\n","per_season[\"podium_share\"].plot(ax=axes[1], color=\"darkorange\")\n","axes[1].axhline(15, linestyle=\"--\", color=\"grey\", label=\"3 of 20 = 15%\")\n","axes[1].set_title(\"Share of result rows that are podiums\")\n","axes[1].set_ylabel(\"%\")\n","axes[1].legend()\n","\n","plt.tight_layout()\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"d7e1c61e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02695,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.285107+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.258157+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Field sizes varied a lot:** the average number of entrants per race was above 20 for most of history: around 25–27 in the 1950s, 25–30 from the mid-1970s to the mid-1980s, and a peak of almost 39 around 1989. It fell below 20 only in the late 1960s (lowest, about 16, in 1969), and it has been exactly 20 in the most recent seasons.\n","2) **The podium share mirrors the field size, as we expected in 1.0:** where fields are large the share is low (lowest, about 8%, around 1989), and where they are small it is high (highest, almost 19%, in 1969). Every race has three podium places, so this is exactly what larger fields should do. In recent seasons the share sits on the 15% line of a 20-car grid.\n","3) **The base rate depends on the era:** depending on the season, between about 8% and 19% of rows are podiums. A model trained on one era sees a different share of podiums than it meets in another, which matters for the data split in Section 3.\n","\n","**What we do next:** part of these large fields are drivers who never started, which the grid-0 view below shows and 1.8 removes. 1.9 redraws this plot after cleaning."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0a66461d","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.027145,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.339074+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.311929+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Qualifying coverage.** In 1.1 we found qualifying data for only 494 of the 1,125 races. Here we see which seasons those races come from."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":31,"id":"5c01fc25","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.395765Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.394671Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.554329Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.553355Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.190443,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.556432+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.365989+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["First season with any qualifying data: 1994\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["races_per_season = raw[\"races\"].groupby(\"year\")[\"raceId\"].nunique()\n","quali_races = raw[\"races\"][raw[\"races\"][\"raceId\"].isin(raw[\"qualifying\"][\"raceId\"])]\n","quali_per_season = quali_races.groupby(\"year\")[\"raceId\"].nunique()\n","\n","coverage = (100 * quali_per_season / races_per_season).fillna(0)\n","print(\"First season with any qualifying data:\", coverage[coverage > 0].index.min())\n","\n","ax = coverage.plot(figsize=(10, 4))\n","ax.set_title(\"Share of races with qualifying data, per season\")\n","ax.set_ylabel(\"% of races\")\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"48a81e24","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.026596,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.609922+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.583326+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **No qualifying data before 1994:** for 44 seasons (1950–1993) not a single race has a qualifying record. The F1 introduction expected the table to begin in the mid-1990s, and our copy confirms 1994.\n","2) **Full coverage only from 2003:** 1994 and 1995 are almost or fully covered, but coverage then collapses: between about 44% and 59% of races in 1996–1998, and below 25% in 1999–2002 (lowest about 6%, in 2001). From 2003 onward every race has qualifying data.\n","3) **This is the qualifying-coverage drift the brief warns about:** a qualifying feature would be missing for every race before 1994 and for most races in 1996–2002. The gaps are not random: whether qualifying exists depends only on the season.\n","\n","**What this means for Section 3:** the F1 introduction gives three options: impute qualifying, fall back to `grid` (which is complete), or restrict the training window to seasons with full coverage. A window starting in 2003 would contain all three splits (train ≤ 2019, validation 2020–2021, test ≥ 2022) with complete qualifying data. We make the decision in Section 3."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0c843d14","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.026797,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.664024+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.637227+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What does a grid position of 0 mean?** `grid` is our main pre-race variable and is complete, but it contains the value 0, which the F1 introduction describes as a pit-lane start and asks us to verify. In 1.1 we also saw drivers who never started the race (`positionText` F for failed to qualify and W for withdrawn). We split the grid-0 rows of each season into those two groups."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":32,"id":"a9e514a9","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.720429Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.719874Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.91295Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.91196Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.224142,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.915489+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.691347+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Rows with grid = 0: 1638 of 26759\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["res[\"grid_zero\"] = res[\"grid\"] == 0\n","res[\"never_started\"] = res[\"positionText\"].isin([\"F\", \"W\"])\n","print(\"Rows with grid = 0:\", res[\"grid_zero\"].sum(), \"of\", len(res))\n","\n","res[\"zero_never_started\"] = res[\"grid_zero\"] & res[\"never_started\"]\n","res[\"zero_started\"] = res[\"grid_zero\"] & ~res[\"never_started\"]\n","grid_zero_share = 100 * res.groupby(\"year\")[[\"zero_never_started\", \"zero_started\"]].mean()\n","\n","ax = grid_zero_share.plot(figsize=(10, 4))\n","ax.set_title(\"Share of rows with grid = 0, per season\")\n","ax.set_ylabel(\"% of result rows\")\n","ax.legend([\"grid 0, never started (F / W)\", \"grid 0, started the race\"])\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"8cacc51f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.027597,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.972427+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.94483+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Most grid-0 rows are drivers who never started:** the blue line (F or W) dominates, with peaks around 21% in the early 1960s, 17–20% around 1977 and 1982, and almost 33% in 1989. After the mid-1990s it is close to 0. In those eras a grid of 0 usually meant \"did not get a starting position\", not a pit-lane start.\n","2) **Grid 0 for a driver who did start is rare:** the orange line stays at 0 until about 1980 and below 1% until the mid-2010s, then rises to about 2–4% of rows from 2019. These are the pit-lane starts the F1 introduction describes.\n","3) **Grid 0 cannot be read as a number:** for a model, 0 would look better than pole position (1), when it actually means the back of the field or no start at all.\n","\n","**What we do next:** the non-starters are removed in 1.8. The pit-lane starts that remain are real races, so in Section 3 we recode their grid as a start from the back instead of leaving it at 0."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7993e727","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02746,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.026948+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:22.999488+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**How often does the same driver appear twice in one race?** The brief requires one row per driver per race, and warns that early F1 breaks this through shared drives. We count the rows whose `(raceId, driverId)` pair appears more than once, per season."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":33,"id":"78b3c6ab","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.08722Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.08671Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.278143Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.277229Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.225136,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.280372+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.055236+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Rows involved in duplicates: 176\n","Duplicated (race, driver) pairs: 85\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 1000x400 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["dup_rows = res[res.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], keep=False)]\n","print(\"Rows involved in duplicates:\", len(dup_rows))\n","print(\"Duplicated (race, driver) pairs:\", len(dup_rows[[\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]].drop_duplicates()))\n","\n","ax = dup_rows.groupby(\"year\").size().plot(kind=\"bar\", figsize=(10, 4))\n","ax.set_title(\"Rows sharing the same (raceId, driverId), per season\")\n","ax.set_ylabel(\"rows\")\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"87541de5","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.031668,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.343228+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.31156+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Duplicates peak in 1953–1956:** these four seasons alone hold 120 of the 176 rows (30, 33, 33 and 24).\n","2) **Almost all fall in the shared-drive era:** 158 of the 176 rows (90%) are from 1950–1957, the years in which the F1 introduction says a team could hand a car to another driver mid-race.\n","3) **A few come later:** 18 rows are from 1958–1978 (6 in 1958, then 2–4 in a handful of seasons up to 1978). They come after the period the introduction describes, so they may have a different cause; we handle them with the same rule as the others.\n","\n","**What we do next:** in 1.7 we look at the 85 pairs in detail and choose which row to keep."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"55029c68","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.03111,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.405449+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.374339+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["---\n","## Part B — Cleaning\n","\n","Part A showed us what is wrong with the raw tables. In Part B we fix those problems one at a time, each in its own step. Every fix is applied to copies of the tables, so `raw` stays exactly as loaded and we can always compare against it.\n","\n","## 1.4 Turning placeholders into real missing values\n","\n","The audit in 1.2 showed that missing values in this dataset are not real missing values. They are written as the text `\\N`, or as empty strings, like the ones we found in `q2` and `q3` in `qualifying`. pandas treats both as ordinary text, so `isna()` cannot see them, and every count of missing data would be wrong.\n","\n","So we replace both with `NaN`, which is pandas' real missing value. We do this on a copy of every table, stored in `clean`.\n","\n","This step only changes how missing values are written. Columns that still hold numbers or dates as text are converted in the next step."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":34,"id":"7ee953b1","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.469707Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.468594Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.707378Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.706316Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.272947,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.709604+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.436657+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[],"source":["import numpy as np\n","\n","clean = {}\n","for name, table in raw.items():\n","    clean[name] = table.replace([\"\\\\N\", \"\"], np.nan)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"b8bb0163","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.027316,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.765836+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.73852+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["To check that this step did what we wanted, we run the audit from 1.2 again on both versions. If the conversion worked:\n","\n","1) **`clean` has no placeholders left:** no `\\N` and no empty strings.\n","2) **`raw` still has all of them:** this shows the original tables were not changed.\n","3) **Each column's missing share is the same as in 1.2:** we only changed how \"missing\" is written, not how much is missing."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":35,"id":"d7bf6050","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.823217Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.822758Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.377004Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.375702Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.586307,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.379221+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:23.792914+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Placeholders left in clean: 0\n","Placeholders still in raw: 160144\n","Missing share per column unchanged: True\n"]}],"source":["audit_after = audit(clean)\n","audit_raw_now = audit(raw)\n","\n","print(\"Placeholders left in clean:\", audit_after[\"n_sentinel\"].sum() + audit_after[\"n_empty\"].sum())\n","print(\"Placeholders still in raw:\", audit_raw_now[\"n_sentinel\"].sum() + audit_raw_now[\"n_empty\"].sum())\n","print(\"Missing share per column unchanged:\", (audit_after[\"pct_missing\"] == audit_before[\"pct_missing\"]).all())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e5d332f1","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.028628,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.43614+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.407512+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **No placeholders are left in `clean`:** every `\\N` and empty string is now `NaN`, so from here on `isna()` counts missing values correctly.\n","2) **`raw` is unchanged:** the original tables still contain all their placeholders, so the loaded data is kept as it was and we can always go back to it.\n","3) **Nothing was lost or added:** every column's missing share matches the 1.2 audit. The conversion changed how missing values are written, not how many there are.\n","\n","**What we do next:** many columns are still stored as text (`object`) even though they hold numbers, dates or lap times. We fix that in 1.5."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7e502079","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02979,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.49405+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.46426+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 1.5 Semantic typing\n","\n","After 1.4 the missing values are real `NaN`s, but many columns are still stored as text (`object`) even though they hold dates, lap times or plain numbers. As long as they are text we cannot sort races by date, compute a driver's age, or compare lap times.\n","\n","\"Semantic\" typing means we convert each column according to what it means, not what it looks like. In 1.1 we saw that `time` means a lap time in `lap_times`, a time of day in `pit_stops`, and a total race time or a gap to the winner in `results`, so one rule cannot fit all of them.\n","\n","We start by listing every column that is still stored as text."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":36,"id":"3e75f584","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.552938Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.552382Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.577502Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.576339Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.057487,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.57978+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.522293+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["circuits : ['circuitRef', 'name', 'location', 'country', 'url']\n","constructors : ['constructorRef', 'name', 'nationality', 'url']\n","constructor_results : ['status']\n","constructor_standings : ['positionText']\n","drivers : ['driverRef', 'number', 'code', 'forename', 'surname', 'dob', 'nationality', 'url']\n","driver_standings : ['positionText']\n","races : ['name', 'date', 'time', 'url', 'fp1_date', 'fp1_time', 'fp2_date', 'fp2_time', 'fp3_date', 'fp3_time', 'quali_date', 'quali_time', 'sprint_date', 'sprint_time']\n","results : ['number', 'position', 'positionText', 'time', 'milliseconds', 'fastestLap', 'rank', 'fastestLapTime', 'fastestLapSpeed']\n","qualifying : ['q1', 'q2', 'q3']\n","lap_times : ['time']\n","pit_stops : ['time', 'duration']\n","sprint_results : ['position', 'positionText', 'time', 'milliseconds', 'fastestLap', 'fastestLapTime']\n","seasons : ['url']\n","status : ['status']\n"]}],"source":["for name, table in clean.items():\n","    text_cols = list(table.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns)\n","    print(name, \":\", text_cols)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"49ca14d1","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.027493,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.644583+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.61709+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","Comparing this list with what we learned about each column in 1.1, the text columns fall into four kinds:\n","\n","1) **Real text, which stays text:** names, short names (`circuitRef`, `driverRef`), URLs, nationalities, driver codes, `positionText` and the `status` descriptions.\n","2) **Dates stored as text:** `races.date` and `drivers.dob`. We convert both to real dates. We need race dates to put races in time order (1.1 showed that IDs are not in time order), and we need dates of birth if we use a driver's age as a feature.\n","3) **Durations stored as text:** the qualifying times `q1`, `q2` and `q3`, the lap times in `lap_times.time`, the pit stop `duration`, and `fastestLapTime` in `results` and `sprint_results`. These are written as minutes:seconds (e.g. `1:38.109`), so `to_numeric` cannot read them. We convert them to seconds. `fastestLapTime` is post-race and never a feature, but it is exactly the brief's example of a lap-time string, so we convert it along with the others.\n","4) **Numbers stored as text:** columns such as `results.position` and `results.milliseconds`. 1.1 suggested they were stored as text only because of the `\\N` placeholder. Now that 1.4 has turned those into `NaN`, they should convert directly.\n","\n","**Columns we leave as text on purpose:**\n","- `results.time` and `sprint_results.time` mix the winner's total time with \"+gap\" values for everyone else, so one conversion cannot handle them. `milliseconds` already holds each driver's total time as a number.\n","- `races.time` and `pit_stops.time` are times of day, not durations.\n","- The session schedule columns in `races` (`fp1_date` to `sprint_time`) give the dates and times of practice, qualifying and sprint sessions. They are filled only for recent races and we do not use them.\n","\n","We convert in that order: dates, then durations, then the remaining numbers."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":37,"id":"ac5a71a8","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.703566Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.703063Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.722313Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.72121Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.052448,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.724659+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.672211+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Race dates: 1950-05-13 00:00:00 to 2024-12-08 00:00:00 | missing: 0\n","Dates of birth: 1896-12-28 00:00:00 to 2005-05-08 00:00:00 | missing: 0\n"]}],"source":["clean[\"races\"][\"date\"] = pd.to_datetime(clean[\"races\"][\"date\"])\n","clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"] = pd.to_datetime(clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"])\n","\n","print(\"Race dates:\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].min(), \"to\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].max(),\n","      \"| missing:\", clean[\"races\"][\"date\"].isna().sum())\n","print(\"Dates of birth:\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].min(), \"to\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].max(),\n","      \"| missing:\", clean[\"drivers\"][\"dob\"].isna().sum())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4f78fae4","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.028096,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.781768+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.753672+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Both columns are now real dates. `to_datetime` stops with an