e small function can read both formats: split the text at every `:` and build up the total, multiplying by 60 each time we move to a smaller unit. `1:38.109` becomes 1 × 60 + 38.109 = 98.109 seconds, and `26.898` simply stays 26.898.\n","\n","The function has no error handling on purpose. If a value has an unexpected format, the cell stops and we see it, instead of the value quietly turning into `NaN`. To prove nothing was lost, we count the missing values in each column before and after converting."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":39,"id":"32a98bd4","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.981116Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.980587Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.802087Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.800997Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.855878,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.804314+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:24.948436+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["qualifying.q1: missing before 156, after 156\n","qualifying.q2: missing before 4647, after 4647\n","qualifying.q3: missing before 6865, after 6865\n","lap_times.time: missing before 0, after 0\n","pit_stops.duration: missing before 0, after 0\n","results.fastestLapTime: missing before 18507, after 18507\n","sprint_results.fastestLapTime: missing before 9, after 9\n"]}],"source":["def to_seconds(text):\n","    \"\"\"'1:38.109' -> 98.109 and '26.898' -> 26.898. Missing values stay missing.\"\"\"\n","    if pd.isna(text):\n","        return np.nan\n","    seconds = 0\n","    for part in text.split(\":\"):\n","        seconds = seconds * 60 + float(part)\n","    return seconds\n","\n","DURATIONS = [(\"qualifying\", \"q1\"), (\"qualifying\", \"q2\"), (\"qualifying\", \"q3\"),\n","             (\"lap_times\", \"time\"), (\"pit_stops\", \"duration\"),\n","             (\"results\", \"fastestLapTime\"), (\"sprint_results\", \"fastestLapTime\")]\n","\n","for table, col in DURATIONS:\n","    missing_before = clean[table][col].isna().sum()\n","    clean[table][col] = clean[table][col].apply(to_seconds)\n","    missing_after = clean[table][col].isna().sum()\n","    print(f\"{table}.{col}: missing before {missing_before}, after {missing_after}\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"aaf10e93","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.028278,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.861021+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.832743+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["The missing counts tell us no value was lost. They do not tell us the conversion is correct. For that, `lap_times` and `pit_stops` give us a built-in answer key: each row also has the same duration in `milliseconds`, recorded independently. If our seconds × 1000 match `milliseconds` on every row, the conversion is right."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":40,"id":"5af2acf8","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.919667Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.919287Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.931192Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.930003Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.043137,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.933113+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.889976+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["lap_times: largest difference from milliseconds = 0.000 ms over 589081 rows\n","pit_stops: largest difference from milliseconds = 0.000 ms over 11371 rows\n"]}],"source":["for table, col in [(\"lap_times\", \"time\"), (\"pit_stops\", \"duration\")]:\n","    t = clean[table]\n","    diff = (t[col] * 1000 - t[\"milliseconds\"]).abs()\n","    print(f\"{table}: largest difference from milliseconds = {diff.max():.3f} ms over {len(t)} rows\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"5b196ca9","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.03035,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.99111+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:25.96076+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["Finally, the numbers stored as text. Instead of typing out a list of columns, we try converting every column that is still text. A column converts only if all of its values are numbers. If even one value is a word (such as \"HAM\" or \"R\"), `to_numeric` raises an error and the column stays text.\n","\n","We deliberately do not use `errors=\"coerce\"`. It would turn any unexpected value into `NaN` without telling us, which is exactly the kind of silent data loss we are trying to avoid."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":41,"id":"4c6ac7f6","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.051841Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.051517Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.132556Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.131182Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.11455,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.135131+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.020581+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Converted to numbers: 10\n","['drivers.number', 'results.number', 'results.position', 'results.milliseconds', 'results.fastestLap', 'results.rank', 'results.fastestLapSpeed', 'sprint_results.position', 'sprint_results.milliseconds', 'sprint_results.fastestLap']\n"]}],"source":["converted = []\n","for name, table in clean.items():\n","    for col in table.select_dtypes(include=\"object\").columns:\n","        try:\n","            table[col] = pd.to_numeric(table[col])\n","            converted.append(f\"{name}.{col}\")\n","        except (ValueError, TypeError):\n","            pass  # contains words, so it is real text and stays text\n","\n","print(\"Converted to numbers:\", len(converted))\n","print(converted)"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"0f8c0214","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.029322,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.198138+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.168816+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Dates:** `races.date` (1950-05-13 to 2024-12-08) and `drivers.dob` (1896-12-28 to 2005-05-08) are real dates, with no value missing. Races can now be put in time order by `date`.\n","2) **Durations:** the qualifying times, lap times, pit stop durations and fastest lap times are now in seconds, and no value was lost: every column has the same number of missing values before and after. In `q2` (4,647) and `q3` (6,865) these are the `\\N` and empty values from 1.2 added together (4,625 + 22 and 6,819 + 46). The lap times and pit stop durations match the independently recorded `milliseconds` on every row (largest difference 0.000 ms over 589,081 and 11,371 rows), so the conversion is correct. `fastestLapTime` has no such answer key, but it is written in the same `minutes:seconds` format that `lap_times` just showed the function reads correctly.\n","3) **Numbers stored as text:** the 10 columns listed above are now numeric. They were text only because of `\\N`, which 1.4 removed.\n","4) **What is still text:** real text (names, references, URLs, nationalities, driver codes, `positionText`, `status`) and the columns we left as text on purpose: `results.time` and `sprint_results.time` (totals mixed with gaps), the time-of-day columns, and the session schedule columns in `races`, which we do not use.\n","\n","**What we do next:** with the right types in place, we check the keys in 1.6: every ID column is unique and complete, and every ID that points to another table actually exists there."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"a31cb101","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.028395,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.254827+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.226432+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 1.6 Key audit\n","\n","The tables only work together if their IDs are reliable. The brief asks for two checks:\n","\n","1) **Primary keys are unique and non-null.** A primary key is the column (or columns) that identifies one row of a table, such as `raceId` in `races`. If an ID were repeated, a join on it would silently copy rows; if it were missing, the row could not be linked to anything.\n","2) **Foreign keys reconcile.** A foreign key is a column that points to a primary key in another table, such as `results.raceId` pointing to `races`. Every value must exist in the table it points to. An ID with nothing behind it (an \"orphan\") would lose its race date, driver or team when we join.\n","\n","**Choosing the primary keys.** Twelve tables have their own ID column, which is the natural primary key. `lap_times` and `pit_stops` have none, so we use the combination that 1.1 showed identifies one row: one lap of one driver in one race (`raceId, driverId, lap`) and one stop of one driver in one race (`raceId, driverId, stop`).\n","\n","The duplicated driver-race pairs from plot 5 in 1.3 are not a key failure: the primary key of `results` is `resultId`. They are a problem with the table's grain (what one row represents), which we fix in 1.7."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":42,"id":"2cdf24e4","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.31322Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.312828Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.400405Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.399601Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.119145,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.4025+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.283355+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Primary keys: 14 of 14 pass\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"datafram