r>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>82</th>\n","      <td>1951</td>\n","      <td>French Grand Prix</td>\n","      <td>647</td>\n","      <td>6</td>\n","      <td>12.0</td>\n","      <td>3</td>\n","      <td>1</td>\n","      <td>own slot</td>\n","      <td>18</td>\n","      <td>R</td>\n","      <td>0.0</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>\n","</div>"],"text/plain":["    year                name  driverId  constructorId  number  grid  \\\n","84  1950    Indianapolis 500       529            150    14.0     8   \n","88  1950    Indianapolis 500       529            113    17.0     9   \n","85  1950   French Grand Prix       627            154    20.0     6   \n","89  1950   French Grand Prix       627            154    26.0    15   \n","86  1950  Italian Grand Prix       579             51    18.0     1   \n","91  1950  Italian Grand Prix       579             51    60.0     7   \n","87  1950  Italian Grand Prix       647              6    16.0     2   \n","90  1950  Italian Grand Prix       647              6    48.0     6   \n","81  1951   French Grand Prix       579             51    24.0     1   \n","92  1951   French Grand Prix       579             51     8.0     7   \n","80  1951   French Grand Prix       647              6    14.0     6   \n","82  1951   French Grand Prix       647              6    12.0     3   \n","\n","    drivers_on_slot     row_type  positionOrder positionText  points  \n","84                1     own slot             31            R     0.0  \n","88                2  shared slot              5            5     1.0  \n","85                1     own slot             12            R     0.0  \n","89                2  shared slot              6            6     0.0  \n","86                1     own slot             15            R     1.0  \n","91                2  shared slot             13            R     0.0  \n","87                1     own slot             17            R     0.0  \n","90                2  shared slot              2            2     3.0  \n","81                2  shared slot             11           11     0.0  \n","92                2  shared slot              1            1     5.0  \n","80                2  shared slot              2            2     3.0  \n","82                1     own slot             18            R     0.0  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# results with their season and race name (results -> races through raceId)\n","results = clean[\"results\"].merge(clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"year\", \"name\"]], on=\"raceId\", how=\"left\")\n","dup = results[results.duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"], keep=False)].copy()\n","\n","# how many different drivers have each non-zero grid slot in each race\n","drivers_per_slot = (results[results[\"grid\"] > 0]\n","                    .groupby([\"raceId\", \"grid\"])[\"driverId\"].nunique()\n","                    .rename(\"drivers_on_slot\").reset_index())\n","dup = dup.merge(drivers_per_slot, on=[\"raceId\", \"grid\"], how=\"left\")\n","dup[\"drivers_on_slot\"] = dup[\"drivers_on_slot\"].fillna(0).astype(int)  # grid 0 has no slot\n","\n","# label each row: grid 0, a slot only this driver has, or a slot shared with another driver\n","dup[\"row_type\"] = np.select([dup[\"grid\"] == 0, dup[\"drivers_on_slot\"] == 1],\n","                            [\"grid 0\", \"own slot\"], default=\"shared slot\")\n","\n","pairs = dup.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])\n","print(\"Rows:\", len(dup), \"| pairs:\", pairs.ngroups, \"| seasons:\", dup[\"year\"].min(), \"-\", dup[\"year\"].max())\n","print(\"\\nRows per pair:\", pairs.size().value_counts().to_dict())\n","print(\"\\nRow types:\")\n","print(dup[\"row_type\"].value_counts())\n","print(\"\\n'Own slot' rows per pair:\")\n","print(pairs[\"row_type\"].apply(lambda s: (s == \"own slot\").sum()).value_counts().sort_index())\n","\n","display(dup.sort_values([\"year\", \"raceId\", \"driverId\"])\n","           [[\"year\", \"name\", \"driverId\", \"constructorId\", \"number\", \"grid\", \"drivers_on_slot\",\n","             \"row_type\", \"positionOrder\", \"positionText\", \"points\"]].head(12))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"6dda65c9","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.029129,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.885096+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.855967+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Shared drives last longer than the 1950s:** the 176 rows form 85 pairs and run from 1950 to 1978, while the brief describes them as \"mainly the 1950s\". 79 pairs have two rows and 6 have three, meaning the driver appears in three different cars in the same race.\n","2) **Most duplicated rows sit on a shared grid slot:** 115 of the 176 rows have a grid position that another driver in the same race also has, 55 have a slot of their own, and 6 have grid 0. A grid slot belongs to one car, so a slot with two drivers is one car driven by two people. This confirms the shared-drive explanation.\n","3) **For 53 of the 85 pairs, the grid tells us which car the driver started in:** exactly one of their rows is on a slot nobody else has. For example, driver 647 at the 1950 Italian Grand Prix has a row on grid 2 that is his alone (retired, ranked 17th) and a row on grid 6 that he shares with another driver (finished 2nd). He started from grid 2 and later took over the car that started 6th.\n","4) **For the other 32 pairs, the grid alone does not decide:** 31 pairs have no row on a slot of their own, and 1 has two. Driver 579 at the 1951 French Grand Prix is an example: both of his rows are on shared slots (grid 1, ranked 11th; grid 7, won), so two drivers swapped cars and each car appears under both of them.\n","5) **Choosing by the result would pick the wrong car:** in both examples the car the driver took over finished better than the car he started in. Keeping the row with the best `positionOrder` would keep the grid 6 and grid 7 rows, attaching a podium to a grid position the driver never started from. It would also choose the row by the race outcome, which is post-race information. We rule this option out."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"eb14e80c","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.029744,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.944083+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.914339+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**Our rule**\n","\n","1) **If exactly one of a driver's rows is on a grid slot nobody else has, we keep that row.** Only this driver is linked to that car, so it is the car they started in. This decides 53 of the 85 pairs using only `grid`, which is known before the race.\n","2) **Otherwise, we remove the driver's rows for that race.** For the remaining 32 pairs, nothing known before the race tells us which car the driver started in. Any guess could put a starting position the driver never had next to the label, and `grid` is our main feature, so a missing row from the 1950s–1970s does less harm than a wrong one.\n","\n","**Rejected rule:** keeping the row with the best `positionOrder`. It chooses by the race result, which is post-race information, and point 5 above shows it attaches a podium to a car the driver did not start in.\n","\n","The rule is applied to `clean[\"results\"]`, so every later step uses one row per driver per race. We check the row and podium counts before and after, and that no duplicates are left."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":45,"id":"fc2cd66e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.003616Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.003302Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.051613Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.049732Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.08067,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.053846+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:26.973176+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Rows: before 26759 | after 26636 | removed 123\n","Pairs kept with their starting car: 53 | pairs removed: 32\n","Podium rows: before 3397 | after 3373\n","Duplicated (raceId, driverId) left: 0\n"]}],"source":["# a pair is clear when exactly one of its rows is on a grid slot of its own: the car the driver started in\n","dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] = dup.groupby([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"])[\"row_type\"].transform(lambda s: (s == \"own slot\").sum())\n","\n","# from the duplicated pairs, keep only the own-slot row of each clear pair\n","kept_ids = dup.loc[(dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] == 1) & (dup[\"row_type\"] == \"own slot\"), \"resultId\"]\n","drop_ids = dup.loc[~dup[\"resultId\"].isin(kept_ids), \"resultId\"]\n","\n","rows_before = len(clean[\"results\"])\n","podiums_before = (clean[\"results\"][\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).sum()\n","\n","clean[\"results\"] = clean[\"results\"][~clean[\"results\"][\"resultId\"].isin(drop_ids)].copy()\n","\n","print(\"Rows: before\", rows_before, \"| after\", len(clean[\"results\"]), \"| removed\", rows_before - len(clean[\"results\"]))\n","print(\"Pairs kept with their starting car:\", len(kept_ids),\n","      \"| pairs removed:\", dup.loc[dup[\"own_rows_in_pair\"] != 1, [\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]].drop_duplicates().shape[0])\n","print(\"Podium rows: before\", podiums_before, \"| after\", (clean[\"results\"][\"positionOrder\"] <= 3).sum())\n","print(\"Duplicated (raceId, driverId) left:\", clean[\"results\"].duplicated([\"raceId\", \"driverId\"]).sum())"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"bcb0a930","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.02941,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.11242+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.08301+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **The grain is fixed:** no (`raceId`, `driverId`) pair appears more than once, so every row of `results` is now one driver in one race, which is our unit of analysis.\n","2) **Exactly the rows we intended were removed:** 26,759 → 26,636 rows, 123 fewer. That is the 176 duplicated rows minus the 53 we kept, one per clear pair. The other 32 pairs were removed completely.\n","3) **The removed rows were unusually successful:** 24 of the 123 removed rows were podiums (20%), against 13% (3,397 of 26,759) in `results` as a whole. This fits what we saw above: drivers often took over a car that was running well, so these rows carry many good results. Keeping them would have attached those results to a starting position the driver did not have.\n","4) **The cost is small and limited to early seasons:** all removed rows are from 1950–1978, under 0.5% of `results`.\n","\n","**What we do next:** the table has one row per driver per race, but not every row is a driver who started the race. In 1.8 we decide which rows belong in the analysis."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"087aa34a","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.030687,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.174302+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.143615+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**A second form of shared drive.** The F1 introduction describes shared drives in two ways: the same driver with two result rows, which we handled above, or two drivers attached to one car's result. The second form does not show up in the (`raceId`, `driverId`) check, because the two drivers have different IDs.\n","\n","A car finishes a race only once, so in the data this form would look like two drivers with the same finishing place (`positionOrder`) in the same race. We look for such rows, and check whether both drivers also have the same grid, laps and status, which would mean they are one car."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":46,"id":"4be6b5fb","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.234312Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.234004Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.265092Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.263763Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.063927,"end_time":"2026-10-19T18:59:27.267179+00:00",