      -0.006   \n","team_form                          0.0                1.0              -0.003   \n","\n","                    std, scale → fill  \n","grid_pos                        1.000  \n","quali_pos                       1.003  \n","driver_rank_before              0.964  \n","team_rank_before                0.971  \n","driver_form                     0.996  \n","team_form                       0.998  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>validation PR-AUC</th>\n","      <th>test PR-AUC</th>\n","      <th>validation F1</th>\n","      <th>test F1</th>\n","      <th>test ROC-AUC</th>\n","      <th>threshold</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>fill → scale (ours, 3.8)</th>\n","      <td>0.736</td>\n","      <td>0.706</td>\n","      <td>0.706</td>\n","      <td>0.673</td>\n","      <td>0.939</td>\n","      <td>0.41</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>scale → fill</th>\n","      <td>0.731</td>\n","      <td>0.707</td>\n","      <td>0.707</td>\n","      <td>0.684</td>\n","      <td>0.939</td>\n","      <td>0.26</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>\n","</div>"],"text/plain":["                          validation PR-AUC  test PR-AUC  validation F1  \\\n","fill → scale (ours, 3.8)              0.736        0.706          0.706   \n","scale → fill                          0.731        0.707          0.707   \n","\n","                          test F1  test ROC-AUC  threshold  \n","fill → scale (ours, 3.8)    0.673         0.939       0.41  \n","scale → fill                0.684         0.939       0.26  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["# alternative order: scale first (StandardScaler ignores missing values when it learns), then fill\n","train_mask = model_table[\"split\"] == \"train\"\n","scaler_first = StandardScaler().fit(model_table.loc[train_mask, FEATURES])\n","scaled_first = model_table.copy()\n","scaled_first[FEATURES] = scaler_first.transform(model_table[FEATURES])\n","scaled_first[\"quali_pos\"] = scaled_first[\"quali_pos\"].fillna(scaled_first[\"grid_pos\"])\n","scaled_first[FEATURES] = scaled_first[FEATURES].fillna(scaled_first.loc[train_mask, FEATURES].median())\n","X_scale_first = {s: scaled_first.loc[scaled_first[\"split\"] == s, FEATURES] for s in SPLITS}\n","\n","display(pd.DataFrame({\"mean, fill → scale\": X_scaled[\"train\"].mean(), \"std, fill → scale\": X_scaled[\"train\"].std(),\n","                      \"mean, scale → fill\": X_scale_first[\"train\"].mean(), \"std, scale → fill\": X_scale_first[\"train\"].std()}).round(3))\n","\n","exp_b = pd.DataFrame([ablation.loc[\"all six features\"].rename(\"fill → scale (ours, 3.8)\"),\n","                      pd.Series(run(\"scale → fill\", X_scale_first)).drop(\"experiment\").rename(\"scale → fill\")])\n","display(exp_b[[\"validation PR-AUC\", \"test PR-AUC\", \"validation F1\", \"test F1\", \"test ROC-AUC\", \"threshold\"]].astype(float).round(3))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"1e03899e","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.111276,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:30.976761+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:30.865485+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **The order changes the training distributions slightly:** with fill → scale every feature has mean 0 and standard deviation exactly 1 on the training seasons. With scale → fill, the columns with gaps end up with a smaller spread (driver standing 0.964, team standing 0.971), because the filled median values are added after the scaling and pull the values towards the centre.\n","2) **The scores barely move:** validation PR-AUC is 0.731 against 0.736, and test PR-AUC 0.707 against 0.706. Test F1 is higher (0.684 against 0.673), but only because the threshold chosen on validation moved from 0.41 to 0.26. With 39 validation races, the chosen threshold shifts easily. This is a threshold effect, not a better model.\n","\n","**Our decision:** we keep fill → scale. It gives exactly the standardised inputs described in 3.8, and the alternative brings no consistent gain.\n","\n","**Summary of Section 5:** removing any one feature group barely changes the scores, because the groups overlap. Neither preprocessing order changes the results noticeably either, so the choices in 3.8 are safe, and they are also the methodologically correct ones."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"6260d032","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.10873,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:31.202082+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:31.093352+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["# 6. Model Explainability (XAI)\n","\n","Section 4 told us *how well* the models predict a podium. This section asks *how* the final model, the neural network from 4.4, reaches its predictions: which features it relies on, and in which direction they push the probability.\n","\n","We use two kinds of explanations, as the brief asks:\n","- **Global** explanations describe the model's behaviour over many drivers: **permutation importance** (how much worse the model gets when one feature is scrambled) and **SHAP** (how much each feature pushes each prediction up or down).\n","- **Local** explanations describe a single prediction for one driver in one race: **LIME**.\n","\n","All explanations are computed on the test seasons (2022–2024), which the model never saw during training or tuning.\n","\n","**Important: an explanation is not a cause.** A feature being important to the model does not prove that it causes podium finishes. It only means the model relies on it to make its predictions. For example, if qualifying position turns out to be the most important feature, that does not show that qualifying well *makes* a driver finish on the podium. A fast car and a good driver lead to both a good qualifying result and a good race result, and the model has simply learned that the two go together. Every result in this section should be read as \"the model uses this\", never as \"this causes a podium\"."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":90,"id":"0a73cd69","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T19:07:31.421993Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T19:07:31.421427Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.29798Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.296654Z"},"papermill":{"duration":7.988833,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.30033+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:31.311497+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["shap version: 0.52.0\n","lime imported OK\n"]}],"source":["!pip install -q shap lime\n","import shap\n","from lime import lime_tabular\n","\n","print(\"shap version:\", shap.__version__)\n","print(\"lime imported OK\")"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"6f7d16fb","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.110517,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.52244+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.411923+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 6.1 Explaining the network in real units\n","\n","The network was trained on the scaled features from 3.8, where a pole position is stored as a value like −1.6. An explanation in those units would be hard to read. We therefore wrap the network in a small function, `predict_podium`, that takes the features in their real units (grid position 1, championship rank 2, form 0.8), applies the same `scaler` that was fitted on the training seasons, and returns the network's podium probability. Every explanation in this section uses this function, so all plots show real F1 values. Because the scaler is only applied, never refitted, the function gives exactly the same probabilities as the network in Section 4, which we check below."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":91,"id":"ecd8d18e","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.747125Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.745188Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.88822Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.887207Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.257198,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.890253+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:39.633055+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Same probabilities as nn_probs on the test seasons: True\n","Largest difference: 5.960464477539063e-08\n","Example, first three test drivers:\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>grid_pos</th>\n","      <th>quali_pos</th>\n","      <th>driver_rank_before</th>\n","      <th>team_rank_before</th>\n","      <th>driver_form</th>\n","      <th>team_form</th>\n","      <th>podium_probability</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>23172</th>\n","      <td>1</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>11.0</td>\n","      <td>6.0</td>\n","      <td>0.0</td>\n","      <td>0.1</td>\n","      <td>0.496</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>23173</th>\n","      <td>3</td>\n","      <td>3.0</td>\n","      <td>11.0</td>\n","      <td>6.0</td>\n","      <td>0.2</td>\n","      <td>0.1</td>\n","      <td>0.352</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>23174</th>\n","      <td>5</td>\n","      <td>5.0</td>\n","      <td>11.0</td>\n","      <td>6.0</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>0.7</td>\n","      <td>0.555</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>\n","</div>"],"text/plain":["       grid_pos  quali_pos  driver_rank_before  team_rank_before  driver_form  \\\n","23172         1        1.0                11.0               6.0          0.0   \n","23173         3        3.0                11.0               6.0          0.2   \n","23174         5        5.0                11.0               6.0          1.0   \n","\n","       team_form  podium_probability  \n","23172        0.1               0.496  \n","23173        0.1               0.352  \n","23174        0.7               0.555  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["def predict_podium(x):\n","    x_df = pd.DataFrame(x, columns=FEATURES)\n","    return nn.predict(scaler.transform(x_df), batch_size=4096, verbose=0).ravel()\n","\n","check = predict_podium(X_filled[\"test\"])\n","print(\"Same probabilities as nn_probs on the test seasons:\", np.allclose(check, nn_probs[\"test\"], atol=1e-6))\n","print(\"Largest difference:\", float(np.abs(check - nn_probs[\"test\"]).max()))\n","print(\"Example, first three test drivers:\")\n","display(X_filled[\"test\"].head(3).assign(podium_probability=check[:3].round(3)))"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4890c901","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.119504,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.124293+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.004789+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **The wrapper is exact:** on all 1,352 test rows it returns the same probabilities as the network in 4.4. The largest difference is 0.0000001, a rounding difference in the seventh decimal caused by predicting many rows at once. The explanations below therefore describe the real final model, not an approximation of it.\n","2) **The inputs are now readable:** the example rows show the features in their real units next to the network's podium probability. This is the form in which SHAP and LIME will show their results.\n","3) **The example rows show the fill from 3.8 at work:** all three drivers have a driver rank of 11 and a team rank of 6. These rows come from the first race of the 2022 season, where no standings exist yet, so the ranks were filled with the training medians.\n","4) **The model combines features rather than following one:** the driver starting 5th gets a higher probability (0.555) than the driver on pole (0.496), because the driver from 5th reached the podium in all of his last five races (driver form 1.0, team form 0.7), while the pole-sitter had none (driver form 0.0, team form 0.1)."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"4966566f","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.110202,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.346941+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.236739+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 6.2 Global explanation 1: permutation importance\n","\n","Permutation importance asks a simple question for each feature: *how much worse does the model get if this feature is scrambled?* We take the test seasons, randomly shuffle one feature column so that its values no longer belong to the right drivers, and measure how much the test PR-AUC (our primary metric) drops. The other five features stay untouched. If the model relies heavily on a feature, scrambling it hurts a lot; if the model ignores it, nothing changes. Each feature is shuffled 10 times with different random orders, and we report the average drop and its spread. The model is not retrained; we only change its inputs."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":92,"id":"0aeb7d0d","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.570663Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.570333Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.561925Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.560422Z"},"papermill":{"duration":6.107401,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.564336+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:40.456935+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Test PR-AUC with nothing shuffled: 0.706\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>PR-AUC drop (mean)</th>\n","      <th>std</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>quali_pos</th>\n","      <td>0.177</td>\n","      <td>0.026</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>grid_pos</th>\n","      <td>0.112</td>\n","      <td>0.019</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>driver_form</th>\n","      <td>0.029</td>\n","      <td>0.017</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>driver_rank_before</th>\n","      <td>0.007</td>\n","      <td>0.010</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>team_rank_before</th>\n","      <td>0.002</td>\n","      <td>0.009</td>\n","    </tr>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>team_form</th>\n","      <td>0.001</td>\n","      <td>0.008</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>\n","</div>"],"text/plain":["                    PR-AUC drop (mean)    std\n","quali_pos                        0.177  0.026\n","grid_pos                         0.112  0.019\n","driver_form                      0.029  0.017\n","driver_rank_before               0.007  0.010\n","team_rank_before                 0.002  0.009\n","team_form                        0.001  0.008"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 800x400 with 1 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["from sklearn.inspection import permutation_importance\n","\n","perm = permutation_importance(nn, X_filled[\"test\"], y[\"test\"], n_repeats=10, random_state=42,\n","                              scoring=lambda model, X, y_true: average_precision_score(y_true, predict_podium(X)))\n","\n","perm_table = pd.DataFrame({\"PR-AUC drop (mean)\": perm.importances_mean, \"std\": perm.importances_std},\n","                          index=FEATURES).sort_values(\"PR-AUC drop (mean)\", ascending=False).round(3)\n","print(\"Test PR-AUC with nothing shuffled:\", round(average_precision_score(y[\"test\"], predict_podium(X_filled[\"test\"])), 3))\n","display(perm_table)\n","\n","ax = perm_table[\"PR-AUC drop (mean)\"].iloc[::-1].plot.barh(xerr=perm_table[\"std\"].iloc[::-1], figsize=(8, 4))\n","ax.set_xlabel(\"Drop in test PR-AUC when the feature is shuffled\")\n","ax.set_title(\"Permutation importance of the neural network, test seasons 2022-2024\")\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"7d8d0bcf","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.114291,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.79804+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.683749+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **Qualifying and grid position matter most by far:** shuffling the qualifying position lowers the test PR-AUC from 0.706 by 0.177 (to about 0.53), and shuffling the grid position lowers it by 0.112 (to about 0.59). These are the only two features whose loss really hurts the model. This matches the F1 introduction, which calls pole position the strongest single predictor of a podium, and it matches the weights of the logistic regression in 4.2.\n","2) **Driver form comes third:** shuffling it costs 0.029. Recent podiums carry information the model uses beyond the starting position.\n","3) **Standings and team form barely matter on their own:** their drops are 0.007 or less, and the error bars cross zero, so the model loses almost nothing when one of them is scrambled.\n","4) **\"Unimportant\" here does not mean \"useless\":** these features overlap with others (heatmap in 3.8: team form and driver form 0.87, driver and team standing 0.88, standings and grid about 0.7). When one of them is scrambled, the model still gets most of the same information from its overlapping partner, so permutation importance gives it little credit. How much each whole group of features adds is measured by the ablation in Section 5, where a group is removed and the model retrained.\n","5) **A caution about grid and qualifying:** the two are almost the same (0.97). Shuffling one of them creates combinations that rarely happen in reality, such as qualifying 1st but starting 18th. Part of their large drop may come from the model meeting such unusual inputs, so their exact sizes should be read with care. Their ranking as the top two features is clear either way.\n","6) **Importance is not a cause:** these numbers show what the network relies on to make its predictions, not what makes a driver finish on the podium."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"95afc52a","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.118271,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:47.029028+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:46.910757+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 6.3 Global explanation 2: SHAP\n","\n","Permutation importance tells us how much the model needs each feature overall, but not in which direction a feature pushes a prediction. SHAP answers that. For every single driver it splits the network's podium probability into a starting value plus one contribution per feature:\n","\n","podium probability = base value + contribution of grid + contribution of qualifying + … (six contributions)\n","\n","The **base value** is the model's average prediction over a background sample of 100 training drivers. A positive contribution means that feature raised this driver's probability above the average, and a negative one means it lowered it. Because the network is not a tree model, we use SHAP's `KernelExplainer`, which works with any model by asking it many \"what if this feature were unknown?\" questions. We compute SHAP values for all 1,352 test rows and show them in a **beeswarm plot**: every dot is one driver in one race, its position shows how much a feature pushed that driver's probability up (right) or down (left), and its colour shows the feature's value (red high, blue low). Features are sorted from most to least important."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":93,"id":"b2a522a6","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T19:07:47.261478Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T19:07:47.260717Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.067384Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.066309Z"},"papermill":{"duration":260.9208,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.06979+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:07:47.14899+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["SHAP values shape: (1352, 6)\n","Base value (average predicted podium probability on the background): 0.184\n"]},{"name":"stderr","output_type":"stream","text":["/tmp/ipykernel_17/2182099988.py:10: FutureWarning: The NumPy global RNG was seeded by calling `np.random.seed`. In a future version this function will no longer use the global RNG. Pass `rng` explicitly to opt-in to the new behaviour and silence this warning.\n","  shap.summary_plot(shap_values, X_explain, plot_type=\"dot\", show=False)\n"]},{"data":{"image/png":"\n","text/plain":["<Figure size 800x390 with 2 Axes>"]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["background = shap.sample(X_filled[\"train\"], 100, random_state=42)\n","explainer = shap.KernelExplainer(predict_podium, background)\n","\n","X_explain = X_filled[\"test\"]\n","shap_values = explainer.shap_values(X_explain, silent=True)\n","\n","print(\"SHAP values shape:\", shap_values.shape)\n","print(\"Base value (average predicted podium probability on the background):\", round(float(explainer.expected_value), 3))\n","\n","shap.summary_plot(shap_values, X_explain, plot_type=\"dot\", show=False)\n","plt.title(\"SHAP beeswarm, neural network, test seasons 2022-2024\")\n","plt.show()"]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"2f75354b","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.114113,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.297022+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.182909+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["**What we notice**\n","\n","1) **The base value is 0.184:** this is the network's average podium probability for a typical training driver. Every driver's prediction starts here and is pushed up or down by the six features.\n","2) **Qualifying and grid position again matter most:** they are at the top of the plot and have the widest spread. Blue dots (a low number, i.e. qualifying or starting near the front) lie far to the right, raising the probability by up to about 0.27 for qualifying and 0.21 for grid. Red dots (starting near the back) lie to the left and lower it by up to about 0.1. Most drivers sit in a tight red cluster just left of zero: for the many drivers starting in the midfield or at the back, these features lower the probability a little, while the big upward pushes are reserved for the few drivers at the front.\n","3) **Form pushes in the opposite colour direction:** for driver and team form, red dots (many recent podiums) lie on the right, raising the probability by up to about 0.17 (driver) and 0.14 (team). Blue dots (no recent podiums) cluster just left of zero. A higher share of podiums is better, so high values help, while for positions and ranks low values help.\n","4) **The standings matter least:** driver and team rank stay within about ±0.06 of zero. Their information largely overlaps with grid and form (heatmap in 3.8), so the network adds little from them.\n","5) **SHAP agrees with permutation importance, with one difference:** both put qualifying and grid first, driver form next, and the standings near the bottom. The difference is team form: SHAP ranks it fourth and shows clear upward pushes, while permutation importance gave it almost nothing (0.001). The two methods ask different questions. SHAP shares the credit between overlapping features, so team form receives part of what it has in common with driver form (0.87 in the heatmap). Permutation importance only measures what is lost when a feature is scrambled, and little is lost because driver form still carries the same information.\n","6) **This shows what the model uses, not what causes a podium:** a front-row start pushes the prediction up because, in the data, front-row starters often reach the podium, not because the model has learned what makes a car fast."]},{"cell_type":"markdown","id":"e9f0b2ee","metadata":{"papermill":{"duration":0.111986,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.52213+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.410144+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"source":["## 6.4 Choosing a prediction to explain locally\n","\n","A local explanation looks at one single prediction: one driver in one race. To avoid picking a convenient example by hand, we choose it with a fixed rule: among all drivers in the test seasons who really finished on the podium, we take the one the network gave the highest podium probability. This is a correct and confident prediction, so it shows most clearly how the model reaches a \"podium\" answer. We add the driver's and race's names from the cleaned `drivers` and `races` tables so the example can be checked against real F1 history."]},{"cell_type":"code","execution_count":94,"id":"3b35aad6","metadata":{"execution":{"iopub.execute_input":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.752162Z","iopub.status.busy":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.751698Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.784286Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.783352Z"},"papermill":{"duration":0.151653,"end_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.786356+00:00","exception":false,"start_time":"2026-10-19T19:12:08.634703+00:00","status":"completed"},"tags":[]},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","output_type":"stream","text":["Chosen prediction: Max Verstappen, 2023 São Paulo Grand Prix\n","Podium probability 0.851 (threshold 0.41) -> model says podium, and he did finish on the podium\n"]},{"data":{"text/html":["<div>\n","<style scoped>\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th:only-of-type {\n","        vertical-align: middle;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe tbody tr th {\n","        vertical-align: top;\n","    }\n","\n","    .dataframe thead th {\n","        text-align: right;\n","    }\n","</style>\n","<table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">\n","  <thead>\n","    <tr style=\"text-align: right;\">\n","      <th></th>\n","      <th>grid_pos</th>\n","      <th>quali_pos</th>\n","      <th>driver_rank_before</th>\n","      <th>team_rank_before</th>\n","      <th>driver_form</th>\n","      <th>team_form</th>\n","    </tr>\n","  </thead>\n","  <tbody>\n","    <tr>\n","      <th>value</th>\n","      <td>1</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>1.0</td>\n","      <td>0.8</td>\n","      <td>0.4</td>\n","    </tr>\n","  </tbody>\n","</table>\n","</div>"],"text/plain":["      grid_pos quali_pos driver_rank_before team_rank_before driver_form  \\\n","value        1       1.0                1.0              1.0         0.8   \n","\n","      team_form  \n","value       0.4  "]},"metadata":{},"output_type":"display_data"}],"source":["test_rows = filled[filled[\"split\"] == \"test\"].copy()\n","test_rows[\"probability\"] = nn_probs[\"test\"]\n","\n","driver_names = clean[\"drivers\"].assign(driver=clean[\"drivers\"][\"forename\"] + \" \" + clean[\"drivers\"][\"surname\"])[[\"driverId\", \"driver\"]]\n","race_names = clean[\"races\"][[\"raceId\", \"name\"]].rename(columns={\"name\": \"race\"})\n","test_rows = test_rows.merge(driver_names, on=\"driverId\", how=\"left